-- EXPECT-ERROR: refinement violation: bound expression does not satisfy type annotation
--
-- A refined `let` annotation that its own bound expression VIOLATES is
-- reported. This is the discriminating half of the pair whose accepting side
-- is `accept/t130_refine_let_annotation_checked_and_composes`.
--
-- `ys` is annotated `{List(Int) | len(_) > 0}` and bound to `[]`, whose length
-- is 0. The annotation is false about the very expression it annotates.
--
-- Until 2026-07-30 this file EXITED 0. `scope_add_binding` admitted an
-- annotated `let`'s predicate into the refined scope channel without ever
-- checking it against `bind_expr`, so `len(ys) > 0` became a fact about `ys`
-- and `inner(ys)` was reported as PROVED -- a proof manufactured out of an
-- annotation the author merely asserted. `--refine-report` read
-- `1 proved, 0 violated, 0 skipped`, and `cap verified`, whose entire premise
-- is "if it compiles, it is proved", accepted the module.
--
-- That made a `let` annotation the one refined position in the language that
-- was believed rather than verified: every other one (a parameter at a call
-- site, a return refinement, an `impl` method parameter) obliges somebody.
-- An unchecked assumption a program states about itself is worse than a skip,
-- because a skip at least declines to claim anything.
--
-- Note the direction of the fix, which the `let-annotation` suite in
-- `test/test_refinecheck.ml` pins by counts rather than by exit code: an
-- annotation the checker can neither prove nor refute is SKIPPED and grants
-- no fact -- it is not reported (that would be a false positive on
-- undecidable code) and it is not trusted either, so no later goal can be
-- discharged by a premise this binding failed to establish.
--
-- No `cap verified` here on purpose: the violation must be reported on its own
-- merits, not as a capability escalation of a skip.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md.
mod T131 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    0
  end

  fn outer() : Int do
    let ys : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0} = []
    inner(ys)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    outer()
  end

end
